.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt PROCESS_MRELEASE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm process_mrelease
.Nd free the memory of a dying process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn syscall SYS_process_mrelease "int pidfd" "unsigned int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn process_mrelease
frees at once the memory of the process
.Fa pidfd
names, a descriptor of
.Xr pidfd_open 2 ,
when the process is dying, rather than when its last thread has ended,
as Linux's call does.
A program that ends others to free memory, such as a daemon that stops
processes when memory is short, sends
.Dv SIGKILL
and then calls it.
.Pp
The process is dying once a
.Dv SIGKILL
has been sent to it or to one of its threads, or once its threads are
ending by
.Xr exit 2
of the group or by a fatal signal; not while its core file is written
or while an exec ends its other threads.
Every other process that shares its address space, by
.Dv CLONE_VM ,
must be dying too.
The pages of every mapping but shared memory and a device object's are
freed, and a thread that touches one before it ends finds it as after
.Dv MADV_DONTNEED .
A second call has nothing left to free and returns 0.
.Pp
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0 on success.
On failure -1 is returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa pidfd
is no pidfd.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
is not 0, or the process is not dying, or shares its address space with
a process that is not.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
The process has been reaped, or no thread of it has an address space
left.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr pidfd_open 2 ,
.Xr process_madvise 2
